\keywords{Interpersonal communication, disagreements, polarization, persuasion, attitude change, behavior change, intellectual humility, implementation intentions}

\begin{document}
\SweaveOpts{concordance=TRUE}

\maketitle

We spend considerable effort avoiding disagreements with the people around us. We steer conversations away from contentious topics, soften our opinions, and sometimes remain silent rather than risk a fight. Yet, the conversations we are motivated to avoid also provide unique opportunities to connect with others who hold opposing views and to critically reflect on our own attitudes and behavior. When the conversation topic is societally-relevant (e.g., topics related to health, politics, or the environment), conversations about disagreements can have profound implications for attitudes, behaviors, and social cohesion; and when the conversation partner is someone we know - a friend, family member, colleague, or acquaintance - the encounter combines counter-attitudinal information with a socially meaningful source of influence that is more difficult to dismiss than a stranger or institution. Across two pre-registered studies, we describe DKOs as an important force of change and social cohesion in society and point towards promising intervention routes that capitalize on this naturally powerful source of social influence. 

Specifically, based on an interdisciplinary literature review and cross-sectional survey we identify the act of planning societally-relevant DKOs as a robust correlate of DKO occurrence as well as attitude and behavior change. We further highlight intellectual humility as a correlate of discourse quality during societally-relevant DKOs and, in turn, outcomes that support social cohesion. In a longitudinal experiment, we subsequently manipulated planning and intellectual humility to test causal predictions about consequential outcomes of real-world, societally-relevant DKOs. 

\subsection{Theoretical Background Study 1}

Research on interpersonal communication in general highlights the frequency [CITE] and immense influence of direct social interactions and relationships across virtually all aspects of human life, including societally-relevant contexts like health, environment, and politics \parencite{jeongEffectCampaignGeneratedInterpersonal2017} [ADD Social Control work ENVIRONMENT AND POLITICS CITESS]. This work highlights a strong association between relational closeness and the strength of social influence. People spontaneously represent and incorporate the responses of socially close others into their own judgments and behaviors \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016}.

Work specifically focused on conversations about disagreements is more sparse and lacks overarching theoretical frameworks and empirical evidence that generalize across topic domains and relationship dynamics. What we do know thus far, suggests that societally-relevant DKOs are potentially impactful, but infrequent, underappreciated, and exceedingly hard to predict for both individuals and scientists. 

To illustrate, experimental research on manipulated political disagreements between strangers shows their potential to impact opinions \parencite{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} and outcomes related to social cohesion such as affective polarization \parencite{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a}. [CITE BAIL, LIEBERMANN etc] Similarly, opinion-leader interventions in which trained persuaders discuss climate change and specific environmentally-friendly behaviors with their social network have shown some promise in changing behaviors \parencite{makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017} [CHECK CITATION]. Observational evidence suggests that when disagreements about societally-relevant topics do occur, they usually happen with known others \parencite{hofstraImpactDyadsExtended2026, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017, jostCognitiveMotivationalMechanisms2022, hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015, moreyWhoMattersTypes2012, schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023,soal}, but that their overall frequency is low \parencite{atariPaucityMoralityEveryday2023, keskinturkWhatAreYou2025,latkinTheyTalkWeather2024}, on average, possibly because of widespread difficulties in predicting their outcomes and the perception that they are unlikely to lead to change. For instance, across three experiments, Wald, Kardas, and Epley \citeyear{waldMisplacedDividesDiscussing2024} found that people systematically underestimate how positive disagreements about divisive topics will be. Experimental studies of conversations between strangers generally confirm this mismatch, with actual experiences rated more positively than expected \parencite{binnquistZoomSolutionPromoting2022}. Related research has also shown that people underestimate how open others are to opposing opinions. Similarly, researchers have reported both successes and profound failures of attempts to intentionally use interpersonal communication to obtain desired outcomes like attitude change or decreased polarization \parencite{DAVID, META_ANALYSIS}. The overall picture painted by these empirical examples remains fragmented across topic domains and research methodologies with key evidence coming from studies on conversations between strangers which stands in contrast to the fact that real-world disagreements are more likely to occur with known others. As such, our first step is to validate these findings in a large sample of naturally occurring, societally-relevant disagreements with known others across three topics.

H1: On average, societally-relevant DKOs are perceived as unenjoyable (H1A), are unlikely to change attitudes (H1B) and behavior (H1C), and do not motivate repetition of similar conversations (H1D).; and
H2: The majority of societally-relevant DKOs occurs between known others rather than strangers. 

\subsubsection{Conceptualizing Societally-Relevant DKOs}

If we can identify robust predictors of both the occurrence of societally-relevant DKOs and their outcomes, we might be able to tap into a large unutilized force for change in society. To this end, we need theories that clearly conceptualize the diversity of potential outcomes of DKOs and link them to relevant predictors in a manner that generalizes across conversations and contexts. We propose a distinction between three key target variables in work aiming to utilize the potential power of societally-relevant DKOs (Figure ~\ref{fig:modelFigure}): 

\begin{itemize}
    \item the frequency with which societally-relevant DKOs occur
    \item DKO outcomes related to attitude and behavior change in one or multiple of the conversation partners 
    \item DKO outcomes related to social cohesion between the conversation partners and, in aggregate, the social groups they represent
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Figure/model.png}
    \caption{Societally-Relevant DKOs: Predictors and Outcomes}
    \label{fig:modelFigure}
\end{figure}

Prior work has acknowledged the relevance of these variables, but focus on a certain target variable tends to be confounded with topic domain, research methodology and field or research group (e.g. \cite{kanterDoesCoupleCommunication2022, anwoerkomRomanceLearningDisagreement2010,hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015,makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017,combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a}). For instance, while frequency has generally been studied using observational methods, little is known about how drivers of DKO frequency relate to the outcomes of specific conversations. In contrast, outcomes have generally been investigated experimentally, often in stranger conversations with unclear implications for real-world discussions with known others. This disconnect complicates both the construction of a holistic understanding of DKOs and the safe design of interventions: increasing frequency without knowing likely outcomes can easily backfire, costly outcome-focused interventions are wasted if the conversations they target rarely occur, and interventions focused on changing attitudes/behaviors can unintentionally harm crucial, pre-existing social relationships if social cohesion effects are negated. 

While we are currently missing a holistic picture of DKO outcomes and their predictors,the existing evidence underscores that predictors of frequency and different outcome types can dissociate, and that these variables must all be measured and targeted intentionally. For instance, while work on conversation frequency highlights emotionality as a key driver \parencite{bergerWhatGetsShared2025, bergerArousalIncreasesSocial2011,stieglitzEmotionsInformationDiffusion2013}, little is known about the outcomes of disagreements that are emotionally motivated. In contrast, work on discourse quality and enjoyment of conversations highlights rationality and respectfulness over emotionality as key factors. Further, while motives to persuade the conversation partner may enhance change goals, communication scientists have highlighted that persuasion knowledge enhances the risk of resistance and negative appraisals of the persuader which can harm social cohesion. In two empirical studies, we examine 1) the act of planning societally-relevant DKOs as a key precurser of DKO frequency and attitude/behavior change that is less likely to harm social cohesion outcomes, and 2) intellectual humility as a precurser of discourse quality and, in turn, social cohesion outcomes (see Figure ~\ref{fig:modelFigure}). 

\subsubsection{Planning and DKO Frequency}

Known natural predictors of the initiation of social interactions (e.g., of online information sharing, word-of-mouth, and general conversation) either discourage societally-relevant DKOs or might lead to DKOs that threaten social cohesion. Prior work suggests that social and self-relevance of a conversation topic are strong drivers of social interactions \parencite{scholzMediaContentSharing2020,cosmePerceivedSelfSocial2025a,cosmeMessageSelfSocial2022}, because they allow the initiator of the interaction to showcase their identity in a positve light and to connect positively to others \parencite{baumeisterNeedBelongDesire1995, bergerContagiousWhyThings2013, bergerWordMouthInterpersonal2014}. For instance, Schmitt-Beck and Schnaudt \citeyear{schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023} identify conflict-seeking disposition and political topic interest as positive predictors of political disagreements, highlighting that people who, on average, find political topics more self-relevant and who perceive lower social costs of disagreements are more likely to engage. 

The strong motivation for positive social interactions makes it easier to choose for topics that, on average, are more often part of daily discourse and that don't cause controversy between the interaction partners. In addition, a well-studied strategy to protect a positive self-image is avoiding counterattitudinal information (CITE XINYAO AS SUMBITTED and MEZULIS). Other well-known drivers of interactions, like arousal, salience, and emotionality  \parencite{bergerContagiousWhyThings2013} may overwrite the self- and social hesitations to discuss disagreements. However, disagreements started in the context of strong emotions may impair careful listening and promote motivated reasoning and, thereby, cause resistance, low enjoyment and relational harm. In other words, natural predictors of social interactions make disagreements unlikely and if they do occur might make them counterproductive. 

We propose that planning a DKO can essentially substitute these naturally occurring frequency predictors and enhance frequency without harming social cohesion. By planning, we mean a concrete and detailed plan to engage in a specific societally-relevant DKO with a specific known other at a specific time. Research on implementation intentions [CITE] and action planning [GOLLWITZER?] suggests that concrete plans can bridge intention behavior gaps through explicit commitment and by supporting the planner's memory with specific situational triggers (e.g., "When I have dinner with X at home tonight, I will ask about Y."). We theorize that planning a DKO increases the chance of occurrence of that specific DKO and might impact overall frequency because one successful/enjoyable DKO might lead to repetitions of similar behavior [MAYBE CITE REINFORCEMENT LEARNING]. Indeed, work on sharing suggests that when given the opportunity and time to consider potential routes through which content could be framed as socially or self-relevant enhances people's likelihood to engage in social interactions about this content even if they wouldn't have done so otherwise \parencite{cosmePerceivedSelfSocial2025a, scholzInvokingSelfRelatedSocial2023}. As such, planning might reduce the perceived self- and social relevance barriers while increasing DKO frequency without relying on the crutch of emotions. Here, we will test the assumption that, given negative perceptions, most people are not already planning societally-relevant DKOs and explore whether those who are more likely to plan DKOs engage in them more frequently.

H3: The majority of DKOs is unplanned and planned vs. unplanned DKOs are  more likely to be repeated.
RQ1: Is planning associated with the frequency of societally-relevant DKOs?

\subsubsection{Planning and Change Outcomes}

There is a strong theoretical basis for expecting interactions with close others to be impactful. Work on the RICOR model \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016} proposes that responses from psychologically close others are spontaneously represented and incorporated into one's own judgments, providing an automatic mechanism for social influence that does not depend on deliberate conformity motives. Longitudinal research on political networks similarly shows that friends and family exert meaningful influence on political attitudes over time \parencite{belloInfluenceMightierSelection2014}. Health psychology and communication research further demonstrates that naturally occurring interpersonal conversations on health and environmental behaviors \parencite{hendriksInterveningConversationsHow2020, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017} CITE SOCIAL CONTROL LIT Yet, the specific conditions under which disagreements with known others produce change remain understudied. 

Experimental research has shown substantial interest in using conversations to induce directional changes in attitudes or behaviors. For instance, research lines on social control have often focused on health-related interactions between romantic partners [CITE URTE SCHOLZ ET AL] and work on political interactions has frequently examined discussions of divisive topics between strangers \parencite{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026}. These studies have manipulated conversational motives, such as persuading or compromising, or varied the strategies used to pursue influence, with mixed evidence regarding their effectiveness [CITES]. However, these approaches have several limitations: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Motive-based interventions are inherently context-specific: they require conversation initiators and intervention designers to identify a particular attitude or bheavior as a desirable target for change. This limits their applicability across the wide range of disagreements that arise in everyday interactions, particularly in societally-relevant topic domains in which there is no clear optimal viewpoint or course of action or where these are actively negotiated over time.
    \item Assigned conversational motives may be difficult to implement credibly in interactions with known others, who are familiar with the initiator’s typical communication style and may therefore recognize experimentally induced motives as artificial. 
    \item Designing interventions around a predetermined direction of change necessarily places researchers in the position of deciding which attitudes or behaviors are worthy of being changed or promoted.
\end{enumerate} 

Here, we propose an alternative route to influencing the outcomes of disagreements: planning. When people disagree with known others, they can draw on a shared history and a rich understanding of the relationship to anticipate how their conversational partner may respond. Planning a disagreement in advance may allow individuals to use this knowledge to calibrate both their arguments and their broader communicative approach to the specific interaction. We therefore propose that planning can increase the likelihood that a disagreement produces attitude or behavioral change, without requiring researchers to specify the direction of that change. 

This approach offers a fundamentally different model of intervention. Rather than functioning as a top-down instrument for promoting researcher-defined outcomes, planning could empower individuals to navigate consequential disagreements in their everyday social relationships more effectively. Moreover, because planning allows individuals to anticipate not only their partner’s responses but also the potential relational consequences of the conversation, it may help them manage disagreements in ways that reduces the risk of relational harm. This approach is valuable in that a society in which there is greater flexibility and fluidity in societally-relevant attitudes and behaviors can more easily adapt to environmental pressures like threats or novel evidence and avoid rigid attitudinal structures like those characterizing polarization. We therefore hypothesize:

H4: Planned vs. unplanned DKOs are perceived to be more instructive (H4A) less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H4B), and more likely to lead to attitude (H4C) and behavior change (H4D).

\subsubsection{Intellectual Humility as a Predictor of Social Cohesion}

Thus far, we have argued that planning enhances DKO frequency and change-related outcomes. While we believe that the preparation can also help planners to avoid relational harm during societally-relevant DKOs (H3), the extent to which they are able to create enjoyable conversations that actively contribute to social cohesion depends on their skill-sets as communicators. We propose that a particularly important characteristic for the creation of social cohesion through DKOs is intellectual humility. 

Exercising intellectual humility refers to recognizing the limits of one's knowledge and being aware of one's fallibility \parencite{porterClarifyingContentIntellectual2022}. While we are not aware of research that has examined the role of intellectual humility in the specific context of societally-relevant DKOs, related existing evidence suggests that trait intellectual humility enables discussants to be intellecutally humble during difficult discussions which leads to increased discourse quality. Higher discourse quality, in turn is associated with outcomes that link DKOs to social cohesion. For instance, Combs and colleagues \citeyear{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a} similarly found that actual cross-party conversations produced substantial reductions in affective polarization, with reductions tied to the civility of the interaction. 

H5: Higher intellectual humility is associated with higher discourse quality during DKOs. 

De Jong \citeyear{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} identified perspective-taking, feelings of inclusion, perceived commonality, and reduced misperceptions as predictors of conversational outcomes. Similarly, Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that exploration and openness during conversations predicted agreement. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} found that high-quality, non-judgmental listening increased interpersonal perceptions and reduced defensiveness. DeMarree and colleagues \citeyear{demarreeLearningGoalsListening2026} found that a goal to listen to a disagreeing stranger rather than to persuade increased perceived psychological safety and, thereby, reduced polarization. These findings converge on the conclusion that higher discourse quality consistently predicts better relational and experiential outcomes, even when attitudinal change is absent: Nussbaum \citeyear{nussbaumEffectGoalInstructions2005} found that a persuasion goal generates more adversarial communication than an exploration goal. Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that a compromise goal produced more agreement than a persuasion goal. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} manipulated high-quality listening in trained canvassers showing that listening improved interpersonal outcomes, but not persuasion. Together, these findings suggest that conversational motives are important, but have only examined the role of experimentally manipulated motives in pairs focusing on conversations with strangers. The effects of naturally occurring motives in DKOs reamin an open question we address empirically.

H6: DKOs with higher discourse quality are perceived to be more instructive (H6A), more enjoyable (H6B), less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H6C) and associated with higher willingness to have similar conversations in the future (H6D). 

